<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Ingredient rationale at a glance">
  <div><strong>Function first</strong><span>Every ingredient should have a clear role in the prepared drink rather than being included only to lengthen the label.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Whole-formula thinking</strong><span>Taste, acidity, solubility, concentration and gastrointestinal tolerance matter alongside individual ingredient physiology.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Claims stay proportional</strong><span>An ingredient can have a known physiological role without proving that a specific finished product delivers a clinical or performance outcome.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Ingredient by ingredient</p>
  <h2>What each component is doing</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>Glucose</h3><p><strong>Why it is there:</strong> carbohydrate plus a substrate for intestinal SGLT1 sodium-glucose cotransport.</p><p><strong>Design consideration:</strong> concentration matters. More sugar does not automatically improve hydration and can make a drink unnecessarily sweet or hypertonic.</p><a href="/pages/glucose-water-absorption">Transport science</a></article>
    <article><h3>Sodium chloride</h3><p><strong>Why it is there:</strong> a direct source of sodium and chloride, the dominant extracellular cation and a major extracellular anion.</p><p><strong>Design consideration:</strong> enough salt changes both physiology and flavour. A practical beverage must balance composition with drinkability.</p><a href="/pages/electrolytes">Electrolyte physiology</a></article>
    <article><h3>Sodium bicarbonate</h3><p><strong>Why it is there:</strong> supplies sodium and bicarbonate and adds bicarbonate chemistry to the formula.</p><p><strong>Design consideration:</strong> it interacts with acidity and should not be confused with much larger performance-supplement doses used in bicarbonate-loading research.</p><a href="/pages/why-bicarbonate">Why bicarbonate</a></article>
    <article><h3>Potassium chloride</h3><p><strong>Why it is there:</strong> supplies potassium and chloride, adding an intracellularly important cation without requiring a separate sweetened ingredient.</p><p><strong>Design consideration:</strong> potassium salts can have a noticeable taste, so inclusion has sensory as well as compositional consequences.</p><a href="/pages/five-essential-electrolytes">Five electrolyte species</a></article>
    <article><h3>Citric acid</h3><p><strong>Why it is there:</strong> controls tartness and acidity, supports a lemonade flavour profile and participates in citrate chemistry when dissolved.</p><p><strong>Design consideration:</strong> acid-base equilibria depend on the final solution, so “citric acid” and “citrate” should not be treated as chemically identical labels in every context.</p><a href="/pages/our-formula">Formula map</a></article>
    <article><h3>Lemon powder</h3><p><strong>Why it is there:</strong> flavour. A hydration product only helps in practice if people are willing to prepare and drink it consistently.</p><p><strong>Design consideration:</strong> flavour is an adherence and user-experience choice, not the central transport mechanism.</p><a href="/products/lemonade-best-hydrate">Current Lemonade product</a></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__split">
