  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Finished-product thinking</p>
    <h2>The formula is a solution, not six isolated ingredients</h2>
    <p>Once powder is mixed into water, the relevant system includes the concentration of all dissolved particles, the drink’s pH, ionic speciation, taste and the volume in which the serving is prepared. Changing one ingredient can therefore alter more than one property of the final drink.</p>
    <p>For example, sodium bicarbonate contributes sodium and bicarbonate, while citric acid changes acidity and reacts within the same acid-base system. Glucose affects sweetness and total dissolved-solute concentration while also serving as a transport substrate.</p>
    <p>This systems view is why we avoid evaluating the formula by a single headline ingredient.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Concentration</p>
    <h2>The amount in the mixed drink matters as much as the ingredient name</h2>
    <p>Sodium-glucose transport, osmolality, sweetness and gastrointestinal comfort are concentration-dependent. The same ingredient can behave very differently when present at a small amount in a hydration beverage versus a pharmacological or sports-supplement loading dose.</p>
    <p>For this reason, preparation instructions should be followed as labelled. Doubling powder without proportionally increasing water changes the entire solution and should not be assumed to create “double hydration.”</p>
    <a class="bhd-page__button" href="/pages/directions">Preparation &amp; directions</a>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Taste is functional</p>
    <h2>A technically elegant formula still has to be drinkable</h2>
    <p>Salt, potassium salts, bicarbonate, acids and glucose all affect flavour. The Lemonade profile is therefore part of formulation rather than decoration added after the chemistry is finished.</p>
    <p>A drink that tastes excessively salty, sweet, alkaline or sour can be difficult to use consistently. We aim for a practical balance: enough flavour and carbohydrate to make the beverage approachable without turning it into a conventional high-sugar soft drink.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Design principle</span>
    <h3>The best ingredient list is not necessarily the longest one.</h3>
    <p>Additional compounds can be useful in the right product, but every extra ingredient changes cost, taste, regulatory complexity and the evidence needed to explain the finished formula responsibly.</p>
  </aside>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">What we intentionally separate</p>
  <h2>Ingredient rationale is not finished-product proof</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Mechanism</h3><p>A known mechanism can explain why an ingredient is reasonable to include.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Formulation</h3><p>A finished formula combines ingredients at specific concentrations and ratios.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Product evidence</h3><p>Testing the actual product is what can establish product-specific outcomes.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Permitted claims</h3><p>Public wording must also remain within the product label and applicable regulatory framework.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__brand-context">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Best Hydrate standard</p>
    <h2>Explain what is there, and do not invent what is not</h2>
    <p>The current formula does not list magnesium, caffeine, artificial performance stimulants or a long vitamin blend. We therefore do not imply that those ingredients are present or necessary to understand the product.</p>
    <p>As the formula and evidence base evolve, this page should evolve with them. Current packaging and the product’s authorized label remain the source of truth for composition and conditions of use.</p>
